## Final Judgement
**Summary:** The fact-checking process has consistently found that no Rafale fighter aircraft crashed on or around September 14, 2020. All evidence points to the viral claim being false, with the shared photographs being reused from a Mirage 2000 trainer crash that occurred in Bengaluru in February 2019. Furthermore, official fact-checking bodies and the Indian Air Force have confirmed that no such Rafale incident took place.

**Missing Information:** All necessary information is present. The retrieved evidence is clear and unanimous in its findings, leaving no ambiguity about the claim's falsehood.

**Conclusion:** Based on the recorded evidence from multiple fact-checking sources and official denials, the claim that "A Rafale Aircraft crashed during training and two pilots are dead" is entirely false. The claim uses unrelated images from a different incident to fabricate a story about a crash that did not occur. Therefore, my decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim that a Rafale aircraft crashed during training, killing two pilots, is entirely false. Multiple fact-checking sources ([The Logical Indian](https://thelogicalindian.com/fact-check/rafale-crashed-formal-induction-ambala-23700), [The Quint](https://www.thequint.com/news/webqoof/old-photos-of-mirage-2000-crash-in-2019-viral-as-rafale-crash-during-training-session-fact-check), [AltNews](https://www.altnews.in/old-images-of-mirage-2000-crash-viral-as-two-pilots-dead-in-rafale-crash-at-ambala/), and [SM Hoax Slayer](https://smhoaxslayer.com/pakistan-creates-fake-indian-twitter-handles-to-show-fake-rafale-training-crash-using-old-photos/)) confirmed that no Rafale crash occurred; the photographs shared with the claim were actually from a Mirage 2000 trainer crash at HAL Airport in Bengaluru in February 2019. The false claim emerged just days after Rafale jets were formally inducted into the Indian Air Force at Ambala on September 10, 2020. The PIB Fact Check officially debunked the claim, stating "no such incident has taken place," and the Indian Air Force denied posting any such announcement.